  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nConvective activity this afternoon looks to be oriented along\na cold front that completed its passage through the forecast\narea earlier this morning. Stronger storms this afternoon will\ntherefore remain out of our territory, but a few lingering\nshowers will prompt one final dousing of rain over the foothills\nand lower Appalachian mountains as the front drifts to the south\nand east.\n\nHigh pressure building in from the northwest will nudge\nlingering overcast southward through the course of the afternoon\nand evening, returning sunshine to most of the forecast area by\nthe end of the day. Webcams on regional road networks show the\nreturn of sunshine across the north-central lowlands, while \nhere in the Charleston metro area we remain under blanketed\novercast. Temperature spread for today will be reflected by\nsatellite trends, with locations under ample sunshine rising\ninto the low 80s and in the 60s/70s for those under lingering\nclouds.\n\nOptimal conditions for fog development will take shape tonight\nwith radiational cooling amid clearing skies. Fog erosion after\ndaybreak Tuesday morning will give way to a mostly dry day under\nthe guise of passing high pressure. Daytime temperatures soar\ninto the 70s along the higher terrain and into the 80s across\nthe lowlands for Tuesday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nUpper level troughing arriving around midweek will work in\ntandem with a closed low over the Mississippi Valley to promote\nactive weather settling back over the region. Increased\nmoisture, coupled with warm temperatures, will yield an\nenvironment conducive for diurnally driven shower and\nthunderstorm development each day for the second half of the\nwork week as a result. \n\nMachine learning probabilities hint at the return of strong to \npotentially severe activity beginning on Thursday and into the \nweekend. This will give rise to the risk of locally damaging \nwind gusts and heavy downpours, which could lead to flash \nflooding concerns. This summertime weather pattern looks to \nprevail through the weekend and into the start of next week \nwhile the longwave trough remains planted over the eastern half \nof the country.",
